<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>16-SEP-2005 09:25 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
Our final day in Australia would be spent right here in beautiful downtown Melbourne.  Being the last day, it was time to pick up a few souvenirs. We had been waiting for this moment because we didn't want to haul this stuff all around with us through Australia.
<p>
M and I walked to the Queen Victoria Market because a tip from the guidebook said it's a cheap place to buy souvenirs.  It was a particularly cold and overcast day with a good chance of rain so we wanted to get our business done as soon as possible and spend the evening just relaxing before the flight out of the country tomorrow as we had an early 6AM flight.  As we arrived at the Queen Victoria Market, we put on our bargaining faces.  That's how things work here. You even bargain with the merchants.  I just want to add, we did indeed get some of the best bargains of the entire trip right here.  The only other good bargain place was up in Cairns. Nothing else in between.  Having obtained exactly what we were looking for, we decided to catch the rest of the sights that Melbourne had to offer.
<p>
The Old Melbourne Gaol (Prison)was first on the list as it was a close walk from the Queen Victoria Market.  Built in the 1800's and infamous for several brutal hangings, it housed some of Australia's worst criminals.  The most famous of them was Ned Kelly.  The place is said to be haunted.  The Gaol was expanded to have a woman's ward which contained all the female prisoners.  Originally, males and females were intermixed in the same wing and of course that caused a lot of bad things to happen to the women if you can imagine.  Prison was all about reform and a belief that a person who had gone awry could be lead back to a proper life through religion. Therefore, all inmates were required to attend the chapel that was built onsite as a part of this reconditioning.
<p>
Walking inside the Gaol, one could feel this eerie, cold feeling.  Perhaps it was the small cells, the thick concrete and brick walls which offered no way out, or the cold stone floors and dim lighting.  M and I entered one of the cells because each cell contained a bit of history as to who stayed there and why they were arrested and when they were hanged.  Unbeknown to us, some grade school kids decided to lock us in the cell which is quite easy to do because all you have to do is close the door and pull on a bolt to lock it into place.  M started to freak out and started shouting and pounding on the door. I got a good laugh out of it because I realized there wasn't much use in freaking out. Instead, I guess you can see we got to experience what it must have felt like to be a prisoner.  Who knows maybe it was the ghosts of the Gaol that locked us in there?  Anyway, someone finally let us out and M stopped freaking out.
<p>
The other eerie thing was that a death mask was made for every prisoner that was hung.  I have to tell you, these things were quite scary looking when you realize they were from the actual heads of people that were just hung.  Eventually, we got to the area where the hangings occurred and you could see the trap door mechanism and where the rope was tied for the hangings.  
<p>
Next stop was on the city tram which was free and SUPPOSE to go all the way around the city.  We hoped it but unfortunately, we were all told to get off as the trains weren't going all the way around the city due to some construction.  Of course, I have to give a negative review of Melbourne's transportation system because not only did they not continue their service in some other form, there was no option but to walk.  Anyway, we got off next to the Parliament building.  Having taken a look outside, we decided to catch some lunch and then hed to Flinders Street Station.
<p>
The walk seemed quite long but we got there and walked into the Flinders Street Station.  Flinders Street Station is one of Melbourne's most recognized sights.  It is the best-known railway station in Australia.  It very much reminded me of Grand Central Terminal in New York.  Several trains merged here and you could catch any of them to go anywhere.  The outside facade of the station has a very Victorian-style architecture which makes it look more like a museum than a train station.
<p>
Right next door is Federation Square.  One of the oddest looking squares I have ever seen, it contains several buildings such as the Ian Potter Center, a racing museum, the vistor center, etc.  However, on the outside of each of these buildings is an awkward facade of non-linear lines and shapes jutting in and out.  Straight lines and 90 degree angles that we are use to, don't exist here.  I really happened to like it.  Something quite novel that I haven't seen anywhere else.
<p>
Right across the street is St. Paul's Cathedral with its impressive belltower.
<p>
The next stop would be to the Fitzroy Gardens which required a good 15 min walk from Federation Square.  On the way there you could see the construction of the new stadium for the 2006 Commonwealth Games that Melbourne would be sponsoring.  Fitzroy Gardens contained a large floral conservatory but most importantly, Captain James Cook's Cottage which was reconstructed there after being transported piece by piece from Yorkshire, England.  Captain Cook, of course, being the famous navigator who discovered Australia.
<p>
The final place we would visit is the Crown Casino Complex, the largest casino complex in Australia.  It basically contained several high end stores, restaurants, and bars, as well as the casino itself and a 5-star hotel.
<p>
Thoroughly exhausted now and with the wind picking up as the storm was coming in, we started to head back to the hostel only to be caught in the rain, but luckily with our umbrellas.  We got back to the hostel where M decided to take a nap, but I was still full of energy and needed to pick up a bottle of Vegemite for Michelle since she requested it.  On top of that, I wanted to try out one last Australian beer while I still had the chance -- Carlton Draught.  It was happy hour time, but all I wanted to do was to find a non-crowded bar to sit in and have a beer.  Unfortunately, it was a Friday night and everyone had left work early to have a drink in a bar.  After walking around for an hour, I gave in and sat in a boring little sports bar that had no windows but was mostly smoke-free watching cricket and rugby as I paid $2 for a glass of Carlton Draught.  
<p>
Sufficiently bored with the bar, I decided to head back to the hostel where I could at least watch a movie or some TV and relax a bit myself before heading back to the room to sleep.  We would have to sleep pretty early tonight so that we could get up at 3 AM to catch a bus to the airport.  
<p>
I was a bit hungry and decided to go check out what might be in the free food bin located in the fridge.  Well low and behold, someone had left some beer...some Carlton Draught beer. Exactly what I had been seeking earlier.  Not able to resist, I took the bottle, sat on a lounge chair, and watched some TV.  
<p>
Afterwards, I was still hungry and decided that with the few dollars I had left, I would go to the nearby 7-11 store and pick up a sandwich, chips, and a drink before I headed off to bed.  And getting to sleep was not hard at all because I too was tired for all the walking around today.  Nothing was going to wake me up, except for my alarm clock, not even the loud dance music that was playing all night long.
